A||||7||PAUL88GT||Speedometer, odometer, and tranny problems ||||5||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||04-13-2001||06:35 PM||||When I bought my 86se recently, the speedometer and odometer did not work. I found out why after changing the oil in the 4-speed manual transaxle.  The mechanisms and gears below the connector/sending unit and oil addition port on the top of the transaxle were missing so the speedometer and odometer were not getting a signal from the transmission.  I guess the previous owner removed them for some reason.<P>My question is what is the best source to get the replacement gears/mechanism that goes into the 4-speed transmission?   I would like to get a used assembly from the salvage yard but, they most likely will not want to sell just the sending unit without selling the entire transaxle.  Also, what other vehicles used this type of transmission so I can expand my search beyond just Fieros?  I believe I have the Muncie 4-speed.<P>Any advice is appreciated.<P>Paul<BR>Red 88GT<BR>Gold 86SE (being restored)   <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||04-13-2001||07:37 PM||||I suspect that the previous owner removed the VSS to check or change the transmission oil and lost the gear.  They are famous for falling off into the transmission.<P>If the gear has fallen into the transmission this could cause problems by jamming on something inside the trans.  I will leave it to others to suggest how to proceed.<P>The gears should still be available from GM or any local speedometer shop.  It is not uncommon to need to swap gears when a significantly different tire and wheel combo is installed on a car. <P>See you at the swap meet in a few weeks!<P>Good luck... <P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||86GT Turbo||04-14-2001||01:44 AM||||Whats up<P>I have a sending unit off one of my forkless<BR>4-speeds <BR>if you want it $20 plus shipping<P>LMK<P>Turbo||||reg||6
Z||000003||PAUL88GT||04-15-2001||05:11 PM||||Thanks Bob and 86gt.  Bob, I do not think it has fallen into the transmission. Shifts good and I have no noise. Just missing. Look forward to meeting you at the swap meet.<P>86gt, I may take you up on that used sending unit if I can't get one at the swap meet.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000004||penetrator||06-10-2001||05:10 PM||||I lost my sending unit gear into the gearbox. It made som really disgusting noise for a while, but then it was eaten up by the other gears. And i dont think it could make too much damage in the gearbox, and i guess thats the reason that the gear is made of nylon or something, and that is also on reason that some gears gets harder and harder by the time, so it can actually crack in half and fall down by itself. <BR>And if the former owner of your car DID take  that gear off, the reason should probably be to make the cars Miles lower, cos that gear counts the milage of your car.<P>(Sorry about the confusing English)<P>Thanks ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bill Osmer||06-11-2001||01:39 PM||||I experienced the above: losing a speedo sensor gear into the the tranny (never to be seen again). The serious problem that resulted was that the DRIVING gear (in the tranny) broke while the tranny was grinding the speedo gear up (Tranny kept on working OK). Before spending any amount of time, you should check if the DRIVING gear is still intact. Remove speed sensor; reach finger into transmission; feel for mating gear (it will be towards the front of the car, and will feel like a gear). If you do NOT feel this gear, then you probably have a bigger problem - I've gone thru this, with the result: now I know how to replace Fiero transmissions!!<P>------------------<BR>Bill Osmer<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumcardsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000006||PAUL88GT||06-11-2001||02:44 PM||||I got a new sending unit from tomanyfieros at Ed Park's Swap Meet.  The speedometer and odometer are working fine now.  I an not sure whether the gear on mine was removed are just came off since I just bought the car in January 2001.  I know that the shaft was only 1/2 of the cylinder. Also, I have since changed the transmission oil. <BR>Paul ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bill Osmer||06-12-2001||01:34 PM||||Paul - I'm glad that you've solved your problem with only a replacement part. As to your changing the tranny oil, there's a lot of electrons on the site recommending what's the proper oil - it's 5W-30 motor oil, and it can be synthetic. The stuff you can buy from Pontiac dealers is synthetic, but Mobil 1 works just great. Don't use gear oil!<P>Bill||||reg||7
